WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:02.760
<v Instructor>So let's talk about SQS.</v>

2
00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:05.040
And at the core of SQS is a queue

3
00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:07.350
because SQS is a simple queuing service.

4
00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:09.000
So we have an SQS queue

5
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.190
and it's going to contain messages

6
00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:12.630
and to contain messages

7
00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:16.080
while something needs to send messages into our SQS queue

8
00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:18.060
and whatever sends a message

9
00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:21.120
into our SQS queue is called a producer.

10
00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:23.520
So it's possible for us to have one producer,

11
00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:24.600
but also have more.

12
00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:27.660
You can have multiple producers sending many messages

13
00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:29.400
into an SQS queue,

14
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:31.140
and the message could be whatever you want.

15
00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:33.690
For example, it could be process this order

16
00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:35.190
or process this video.

17
00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:38.400
Whatever message you create goes into the queue.

18
00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:41.700
Then something needs to process the messages from the queue

19
00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:42.930
and receive them.

20
00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:44.640
And it's called consumers.

21
00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.460
So consumers will pull the messages from the queue.

22
00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:49.650
So that means that they will ask the queue,

23
00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:51.000
do you have any message for me?

24
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:52.890
And the queue says, yes, here it is.

25
00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:54.630
And the consumer will pull these messages

26
00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:56.250
and get some information.

27
00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:58.680
And then with that message, it will process it

28
00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:00.660
and then delete it back from the queue.

29
00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:03.180
And you may have multiple consumers consuming messages

30
00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:04.680
from an SQS queue.

31
00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:08.940
So queuing service is here to be a buffer to decouple

32
00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:12.000
between your producers and your consumers.

33
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.160
Now, SQS is a complicated service

34
00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:15.330
and we'll see it in depth,

35
00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:18.690
but the very first offering is called Amazon SQS

36
00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:20.460
for standard queues.

37
00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:24.630
And SQS, historically on AWS, it is the oldest offering.

38
00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:27.360
It was one of the first services on AWS.

39
00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:28.530
It's over 10 years old,

40
00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:31.680
so it's quite, quite set in stone about how it works.

41
00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:33.060
It's a fully managed service

42
00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:35.520
and it will be used to decouple applications.

43
00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:39.330
So anytime you see application decoupling in your exam,

44
00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:41.880
think about Amazon SQS.

45
00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:44.743
Now, why is SQS so special?

46
00:01:44.743 --> 00:01:47.400
Well, we get an unlimited throughput.

47
00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:49.500
That means you can send as many messages per second

48
00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:50.640
as you want,

49
00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:53.700
and the queue can have as many messages as you want as well.

50
00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:55.410
So there's no limit on throughput

51
00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:58.740
and no limit in the number of messages in the queue.

52
00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:00.930
Now each message is short-lived.

53
00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:01.800
What does that mean?

54
00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:03.330
That means that by default,

55
00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:06.807
the message will stay in the queue for four days.

56
00:02:06.807 --> 00:02:08.550
And the maximum amount of time

57
00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:11.880
that the message can be in a queue is 14 days.

58
00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:14.400
That means that as soon as you send a message to a queue,

59
00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.680
it has to be read by a consumer

60
00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:19.320
and deleted from the queue after being processed

61
00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:21.450
within that retention period.

62
00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:23.370
Otherwise it will be lost.

63
00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:25.020
Then we have low latency.

64
00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:27.990
So SQS means that that means that

65
00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:30.360
whenever you send a message or read a message from SQS,

66
00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:32.040
you will get a response very quickly,

67
00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:34.770
less than 10 milliseconds on publish and receive.

68
00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:37.050
And the messages in SQS, they have to be small,

69
00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:40.350
they have to be less than 1,024 kilobytes

70
00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:41.910
per message is sent.

71
00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:43.860
Now, SQS is a queuing service, as you can see,

72
00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:45.870
high throughput, high volume and so on.

73
00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:49.470
And so it is possible to have duplicates messages.

74
00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:50.790
That means that, for example,

75
00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:53.280
sometimes a message will be delivered twice.

76
00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:56.100
And so this is why it's called at least once delivery.

77
00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:58.890
And if you go ahead with writing an application,

78
00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:00.900
you need to take that into account.

79
00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:03.720
It can also have out of the order messages,

80
00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:06.480
which means it's best effort ordering.

81
00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:09.660
And we'll see there is another type of offering from SQS

82
00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:11.460
that can deal with that limitation.

83
00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:13.770
But we'll see this later on in this section.

84
00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:16.830
So let's go back to our message producers.

85
00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:21.150
So messages are sent into SQS by producers.

86
00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:23.220
And how do they, how does it happen?

87
00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:25.590
Well, the producers will send the messages to SQS

88
00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:28.560
using an SDK, software development kits,

89
00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:31.170
and the API to send a message to SQS

90
00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:34.080
is called SendMessage, very simple.

91
00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:36.150
Now, the message, it will be written,

92
00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:38.520
it will be persisted into the SQS queue

93
00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:42.150
until a consumer reads that message and deletes it,

94
00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:45.210
which signifies that the message has been processed.

95
00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:46.380
We know the retention.

96
00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:48.450
And so what's the use case for producing messages?

97
00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:51.600
For example, you want to process an order, for example,

98
00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:54.180
packet and then ship it to the sender.

99
00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:56.040
And so you wanna do this in your own time.

100
00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:58.770
So you will send a message into the SQS queue

101
00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:01.740
with maybe some information such as the order id,

102
00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:04.470
the customer id, and any attributes you may want,

103
00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:06.090
for example, the address and so on.

104
00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:08.490
And then your consumer, that is an application you write,

105
00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:11.520
will have to deal with that message itself.

106
00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:12.750
So again, to confirm,

107
00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:16.590
SQS standard held has unlimited throughputs.

108
00:04:16.590 --> 00:04:18.870
So we've seen about producers, very simple.

109
00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:21.030
Now let's look about consumers.

110
00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:23.280
So consumers, they are applications

111
00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:25.410
that you have to write with some code.

112
00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:29.190
And these applications can be running on EC2 instances,

113
00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:31.770
so your virtual servers on AWS,

114
00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:33.210
but also they can be running

115
00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:36.630
on your own on-premises servers if you wanted to,

116
00:04:36.630 --> 00:04:38.250
or we haven't seen it yet.

117
00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:41.790
But they can also be running on Lambda functions

118
00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:42.840
on AWS Lambda.

119
00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:43.673
We'll see in this course

120
00:04:43.673 --> 00:04:46.470
it is a serverless compute type of service.

121
00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:48.810
Well, this means that you can just read messages

122
00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:49.920
directly from Lambda as well.

123
00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:51.360
We'll see this later on this course.

124
00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:52.290
Do not worry.

125
00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:56.970
So going back to our simple use case about EC2 instances,

126
00:04:56.970 --> 00:04:59.460
our queue has a consumer

127
00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:03.057
and the consumer pulls for SQS for messages.

128
00:05:03.057 --> 00:05:05.550
And that means that the consumer will ask the queue,

129
00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:07.650
do you have messages from me?

130
00:05:07.650 --> 00:05:10.890
And the consumer may receive up to 10 messages at a time.

131
00:05:10.890 --> 00:05:13.740
So if there are messages in the SQS queue,

132
00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:15.210
it will receive a valid response saying,

133
00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:18.390
here are the messages that are waiting for you.

134
00:05:18.390 --> 00:05:20.400
Then the consumer, it's your code,

135
00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:22.890
has a responsibility to process these messages.

136
00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:26.370
For example, insert some orders into an RDS database.

137
00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:28.920
So you will go ahead and for each order,

138
00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:31.170
you will insert it into your RDS database,

139
00:05:31.170 --> 00:05:33.630
something that you have to write obviously with your code.

140
00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:36.480
And then because these messages have been processed,

141
00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:37.740
because they have been received

142
00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:40.590
and inserted into an Amazon RDS database,

143
00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:43.440
your consumer will go ahead and delete these messages

144
00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:46.590
from the queue using the delete message API.

145
00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:49.260
And this will guarantee that no other consumer

146
00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:50.880
will be able to see these messages,

147
00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:54.243
and therefore the message processing is complete.

148
00:05:55.140 --> 00:05:57.090
So now we can scale this up.

149
00:05:57.090 --> 00:05:59.250
We can have multiple consumers at a time.

150
00:05:59.250 --> 00:06:01.533
So SQS queue can have multiple consumers

151
00:06:01.533 --> 00:06:05.550
that will receive and process these messages in parallel.

152
00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:07.950
So here we are three on EC2 instances.

153
00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:10.860
And so each consumer will receive a different set

154
00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:13.830
of messages by calling the poll function.

155
00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:16.980
And so if somehow a message is not processed fast enough

156
00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:19.920
by consumer, it will be received by other consumers.

157
00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:23.340
And so this is why we have at least once delivery,

158
00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:26.760
and this is also why we have best effort message ordering.

159
00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:28.620
Now, as I said, when the consumers are done

160
00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:30.300
with the messages, they will have to delete them,

161
00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:33.330
otherwise other consumers will see these messages.

162
00:06:33.330 --> 00:06:35.580
And so what that means is that with SQS gueues,

163
00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:38.353
if we need to increase the throughputs

164
00:06:38.353 --> 00:06:40.260
because we have more messages,

165
00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:43.740
then we can add consumers and do horizontal scaling

166
00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:46.620
to improve the throughput of processing.

167
00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:49.080
And so if you remember what we said,

168
00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:51.810
this is a perfect use case for using SQS

169
00:06:51.810 --> 00:06:54.120
with your auto scaling groups or ASG.

170
00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:55.440
So what does that mean?

171
00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:58.200
Well, that means that your consumers will be running

172
00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:01.440
on EC2 instances inside of an auto scaling group,

173
00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:04.800
and they will be pulling for messages from the SQS queue.

174
00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:07.350
But now your auto scaling group has to be scaling

175
00:07:07.350 --> 00:07:09.090
on some kind of metric.

176
00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:12.510
And a metric that is available to us is the queue length.

177
00:07:12.510 --> 00:07:15.060
It's called approximate number of messages.

178
00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:16.260
It is a CloudWatch metric

179
00:07:16.260 --> 00:07:18.900
that's available on any SQS queue.

180
00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:21.570
And we could set up an alarm such as whenever

181
00:07:21.570 --> 00:07:25.051
the queue length go over a certain level,

182
00:07:25.051 --> 00:07:27.570
then please set up a CloudWatch alarm.

183
00:07:27.570 --> 00:07:30.960
And this alarm should increase the capacity

184
00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:34.020
of my auto scaling group by X amount.

185
00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:36.420
And this will guarantee that the more messages

186
00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:37.500
you have in your SQS queue,

187
00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:40.500
maybe because there's a surge of orders on your websites,

188
00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:42.930
the more easy two instances will be provided

189
00:07:42.930 --> 00:07:44.400
by your auto scaling group

190
00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:46.800
and you will accordingly process these messages

191
00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:48.450
at a higher throughput.

192
00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:50.160
So this is a very common integration

193
00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:52.440
that you will see in the exam.

194
00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:53.940
Now, SQS, again,

195
00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:57.750
the use case is to decouple between applications.

196
00:07:57.750 --> 00:07:59.310
So application tiers.

197
00:07:59.310 --> 00:08:01.950
So for example, let's take an example of an application

198
00:08:01.950 --> 00:08:03.780
that processes videos.

199
00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:07.260
We could have just one big application that call

200
00:08:07.260 --> 00:08:09.480
that's called the front end that will take the request

201
00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:11.640
and whenever a video needs to be processed,

202
00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:12.630
it will do the processing

203
00:08:12.630 --> 00:08:15.090
and then insert that into an S3 buckets.

204
00:08:15.090 --> 00:08:17.190
But the problem is that processing may be very,

205
00:08:17.190 --> 00:08:20.940
very long to do and it may just slow down your websites

206
00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:23.610
if you do this in the front end tier.

207
00:08:23.610 --> 00:08:25.920
So instead, you can decouple your application tier

208
00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:27.390
and say, wait a minute,

209
00:08:27.390 --> 00:08:29.580
the request of processing a file

210
00:08:29.580 --> 00:08:31.380
and the actual processing of a file

211
00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:33.480
can happen in two different applications.

212
00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:36.690
And therefore whenever you take a request to process a file,

213
00:08:36.690 --> 00:08:39.900
you will send a message into an SQS queue.

214
00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:42.480
Now when you do the request to process

215
00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:44.490
that file will be in the SQS queue,

216
00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:47.370
and you can create a second processing tier

217
00:08:47.370 --> 00:08:49.410
called the backend processing application

218
00:08:49.410 --> 00:08:51.510
that will be in its own auto scaling group

219
00:08:51.510 --> 00:08:54.450
to receive these messages, process these videos,

220
00:08:54.450 --> 00:08:57.030
and insert them into industry buckets.

221
00:08:57.030 --> 00:08:59.280
So as we can see here with this architecture,

222
00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:01.860
we can scale the front end accordingly

223
00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:04.260
and we can scale the backend accordingly as well,

224
00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:05.670
but independently.

225
00:09:05.670 --> 00:09:08.700
And because the SQS queue has unlimited throughput

226
00:09:08.700 --> 00:09:10.740
and it has unlimited number of messages

227
00:09:10.740 --> 00:09:13.590
in terms of the queue, then you are really safe.

228
00:09:13.590 --> 00:09:17.550
And this is a robust and scalable type of architecture.

229
00:09:17.550 --> 00:09:19.290
And also for your front end,

230
00:09:19.290 --> 00:09:22.290
you can use the optimal type of EC2 instances

231
00:09:22.290 --> 00:09:24.780
or architecture for your front end and for the backend,

232
00:09:24.780 --> 00:09:26.370
maybe if you're doing some video processing,

233
00:09:26.370 --> 00:09:30.150
you can use some EC2 instances that have a GPU,

234
00:09:30.150 --> 00:09:32.310
a graphical processing unit because you know

235
00:09:32.310 --> 00:09:34.170
that these type of instances will be optimal

236
00:09:34.170 --> 00:09:35.760
for doing this kind of workload.

237
00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:37.950
So this is the kind of architecture

238
00:09:37.950 --> 00:09:39.090
that will come up in the exam

239
00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:40.200
and that you're expected to know.

240
00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:41.220
And this is an amazing

241
00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:44.370
and tremendous use case for SQS queues.

242
00:09:44.370 --> 00:09:46.230
Finally, SQS security.

243
00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:48.870
So we have encryption in flight by sending

244
00:09:48.870 --> 00:09:51.570
and producing messages using the HTTPS API.

245
00:09:51.570 --> 00:09:54.180
We could add at-rest encryption using KMS keys.

246
00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:56.700
And if we wanted to, we can do client side encryption,

247
00:09:56.700 --> 00:09:58.440
but that means that the client has to perform

248
00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:00.210
the encryption and the decryption itself.

249
00:10:00.210 --> 00:10:03.510
It's not something that's supported by SQS out of the box.

250
00:10:03.510 --> 00:10:04.800
For access controls,

251
00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:07.530
IAM policies are going to be able to regulate access

252
00:10:07.530 --> 00:10:12.060
to the SQS API, but we also have SQS access policies,

253
00:10:12.060 --> 00:10:14.430
which are similar to S3 bucket policies,

254
00:10:14.430 --> 00:10:15.840
and they're very helpful when you want

255
00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:18.540
to do cross account access to SQS queue

256
00:10:18.540 --> 00:10:21.180
or when you want to allow other services such as SNS

257
00:10:21.180 --> 00:10:22.350
that we'll see very soon,

258
00:10:22.350 --> 00:10:25.140
or Amazon S3 to write to an SQS queue,

259
00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:27.240
for example, with S3 events.

260
00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:29.100
So that's it for SQS for an overview.

261
00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:29.933
I hope you liked it,

262
00:10:29.933 --> 00:10:32.730
and I will see you in the next lecture for some practice.

